  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThe weather this forecast package continues to look benign with near \nnormal (today) to above normal temperatures (the rest of the \nforecast) and no hazards expected. This morning will again get off \nto chilly start with temperatures in the upper 30s to lower 40s. \nToday, the upper level flow becomes quasi-zonal with modest WAA \ngetting underway with southerly winds between 10 to 15 mph with \ngusts to 25 mph. This will aid in highs returning to near normal in \nthe mid 60s to near 70. Monday, a upper level trough will begin down \nthe west coast. In response, upstream upper level ridging will begin \nto build over the central CONUS. WAA will not be as strong on Monday \nas a surface ridge extends from the Great Lakes back into the mid-\nMississippi River Valley however, height rises will again help \nthe area reach the mid 60s to near 70s. Tuesday and Wednesday \nthe upper level trough will continue to dig south down the west \ncoast further amplifying upper level ridging over the central \nCONUS. This will continue the warming trend with highs reaching \nthe mid 70s by Wednesday. Thursday the upper level trough will \nmove into the California coast and move northeastward into the \ncentral Rockies on Friday. This will allow the upstream upper \nlevel ridge to continue to amplify as the axis move directly \nover the local area. This will continue to push highs into the \nupper 70s to lower 80s Thursday and Friday. Saturday, a weakened\nupper level trough will move into the northern Plains. This \nwill force a weak cold front towards the area Saturday which \nwill bring a slight chance (15-25%) of showers for areas along \nand west of I-35. With the cold front struggling to push into \nthe area highs Saturday will still achieve the mid 70 to lower \n80s.",
